Z||000010||Tom88gt||12-29-1999||01:04 PM||||Check with PISA.  I have a catalog from a couple of years ago and it had a TARGA TOP kit for the Fiero, complete with the subframe bracing required to maintain rigidity.  It was offered at $1,900 $1,995 or something like that.  Just under the 2K mark.  <P>I would rather have a full targa over t-tops any day so I wouldn't value the t-tops anywhere close to 2 grand extra on a  Fiero.  I have a t-top myself, it's nice and if you can find a t-top Fiero for a reasonable premium get it.  Otherwise do the targa kit.  But that's my personnal opinion.  <P>If you want t-tops your going to have to buy it already on the car, or find a t-top in a junkyard.  C&C is out of business (they did all the t-tops, even the "factory '88s").  <P>As with the '88 suspension, t-tops are unique, read "expensive", to maintain.    Parts are not as readily available.  The benefit of having them must be weighed against the increased cost of caring for them.<P>Remember, although 370,000+ Fieros were made.  This forum has less than 700 members.  The bad press Fiero's received while in production doomed the car and resale values forever.  For every Fiero which has a loving home, there may be 500 who are homeless/abused/unwanted/R.I.P.  Sad but true.<P>Ultimately, what is it worth to you?||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||12-29-1999||01:09 PM||||When I was doing my clutch replacement I found myself standing/sitting in the trunk quite often. While working on the car, a friend suggested seeing if I could fit in the trunk and close the lid. I could. I had to bend and twist quite a bit, but I fit with the lid closed. I'm 5'11" maybe 6', and 155lbs. It was a tight fit. ||||reg||10
